# Keep the build context lean AND stable. Both the run-container builds
# (containers/*/Dockerfile — the shared tooling builder in containers/tools/, which
# compiles every asset binary from crates/, plus the adversarial and performance
# images, which compile the Foray/Lattice tooling from the engines submodule in their
# own stages) and the
# service-image builds (deployments/images/services.Dockerfile, which compiles the
# backend/auth/dispatcher/driver/artifacts/arena/publisher crates) use the
# repository root as their context. Almost every one of those builds is just a `cargo
# build` over the Rust workspace — nothing else — so this is an ALLOWLIST: ignore
# everything, then re-include only the paths cargo actually reads.
#
# (The per-asset-kind images — sprite, voxel, mc, … — no longer read the context at
# all: they only `COPY --from` the tooling builder or a `tool-*` repository's image.
# They are still built with the
# repo root as their context for consistency, and it costs them nothing because they
# read none of it.)
#
# WHAT THIS ALLOWLIST ACTUALLY WEIGHS, because this paragraph used to say "~100kB" and
# was wrong by three orders of magnitude for years: it was ~35 MB across ~2,300 files
# while gg was built here, ~13 MB of them gg's guest packages, which left with gg. Nearly
# all of what is left is the Rust sources (the crates of the superrepo, contracts and
# engines are ~15 MB without their test data) — real build inputs, every one of them
# compiled by some image.
# The figure is worth keeping honest because it is the unit of the `COPY . .` snapshot
# each of the seven whole-context builds materialises on a cache miss, which is why the
# re-exclusions at the foot of this file keep ignored output out of it.
#
# Flipping to an allowlist (rather than enumerating things to exclude) means a
# change to anything cargo does NOT compile — the frontend, the docs, test cases,
# k8s manifests, models — can never invalidate a build layer. Only a change to a
# file in the list below busts the `COPY . .` cache, which is the whole point: those
# edits genuinely shouldn't trigger an image rebuild.

# SUBMODULES. A path in a submodule is re-included like any other, by naming it (the
# submodule's own path, or a path inside it, never a wildcard), and `*` below keeps
# every submodule this file does not name out of the context, cold-storage's ~2 GB
# of baseline media included. contracts, engines, gg, microservice-repos,
# tool-model-core, tool-paint, tool-particle, tool-sprite, tool-voxel, tool-mc, tool-sn,
# tool-dc, test-suites and ui-common are the ones named, below. What a submodule path
# adds is a precondition: the build reads what the checkout it runs in holds, and a
# checkout leaves every submodule empty until it is initialized. So the image jobs run
# `scripts/ci/submodules.sh init --build-context`, which initializes exactly the
# submodules this file admits any part of (`scripts/ci/build-context.sh
# --submodules` prints them), and a local image build needs the same first. The
# build-context gate checks a re-included path inside a submodule against that
# submodule's checkout, and names it as unverified where the checkout has none.

# Ignore everything by default…
*

# …then re-include the Rust workspace and the few files a build embeds. All
# workspace members live under crates/. The workspace names the contracts and
# engines repositories' crates by their public sources, and the patch table in
# .cargo/ sends each source to its submodule, whose crates inherit their version and
# dependencies from its own workspace manifest, so cargo reads that too. cargo loads
# every patch path, a table nothing builds from included, so every checked-out
# repository the table names is re-included here (microservice-repos,
# tool-model-core, tool-paint, tool-particle, tool-sprite, tool-voxel, tool-mc, tool-sn,
# tool-dc, ui-common). This is the complete set cargo needs to resolve and compile any
# crate; resolving the lock still fetches the patched sources, the public mirrors, over
# the network.
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/rust-toolchain.toml
!/.cargo
!/crates
!/contracts/Cargo.toml
!/contracts/crates
!/engines/Cargo.toml
!/engines/crates
!/microservice-repos/Cargo.toml
!/microservice-repos/crates
!/tool-model-core/Cargo.toml
!/tool-model-core/crates
!/tool-paint/Cargo.toml
!/tool-paint/crates
!/tool-particle/Cargo.toml
!/tool-particle/crates
!/tool-sprite/Cargo.toml
!/tool-sprite/crates
!/tool-voxel/Cargo.toml
!/tool-voxel/crates
!/tool-mc/Cargo.toml
!/tool-mc/crates
!/tool-sn/Cargo.toml
!/tool-sn/crates
!/tool-dc/Cargo.toml
!/tool-dc/crates
!/ui-common/Cargo.toml
!/ui-common/crates
# crates/driver and the engines repository's crates/engines bake these three trees
# into the binary verbatim via include_str! (the driver the built-in orchestrator
# runner scripts and the harness manifests, engines the built-in engine manifests,
# engines/engines/<slug>/engine.toml), so they must be present in the context even
# though they are not Rust sources. A tree that is baked in but not re-included here
# does not fail at COPY like a missing Dockerfile source does — it fails much later,
# as `error: couldn't read ...: No such file or directory` from the compiler, which
# is how the engine manifests were missed when engines landed.
!/orchestrators
!/harnesses
!/engines/engines
# The gg repository's crate, which no image compiles: the patch table in .cargo/ names
# it like every repository's crate, and cargo loads every patch path eagerly, so a build
# under the superrepo fails without it. Cargo reads its manifest, the gg workspace
# manifest that manifest inherits from, and the three target files it names, and records
# the entry in the lock as `[[patch.unused]]`; nothing more of gg enters a `COPY . .`.
!/gg/Cargo.toml
!/gg/crates/gg/Cargo.toml
!/gg/crates/gg/build.rs
!/gg/crates/gg/src/lib.rs
!/gg/crates/gg/src/main.rs

# The backend and driver service images (services.Dockerfile, `--target backend`
# and `--target driver`) bake contracts' Playwright browser-driver into their
# runtime, the backend to render references at ingest and the driver to validate,
# so the driver script, the sibling ES modules it imports (its reporter-side `ttc`
# kit and the `validation` runtime), and its manifest must survive the allowlist
# too. Any file driver.mjs imports by relative path must be re-included here AND
# copied by the image Dockerfiles, or a render dies with a runtime
# ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND. (These are the one non-cargo exception above; editing any
# of them does bust the two images' COPY cache, but they change very rarely.)
!/contracts/packages/browser-driver/package.json
!/contracts/packages/browser-driver/driver.mjs
!/contracts/packages/browser-driver/validation.mjs
!/contracts/packages/browser-driver/ttc.mjs

# The adversarial and performance run-container images assemble a source-only
# "buildkit" — the verbatim engine crates plus a de-workspaced root Cargo.toml that
# re-supplies their `workspace = true` inheritance — which they `cp` from the build
# context and smoke-build standalone (see containers/{adversarial,performance}/Dockerfile).
# The crates ride in under !/engines/crates, but these de-workspaced root manifests
# live outside it, so re-include them explicitly or the COPY/cp stages can't see them.
!/containers/adversarial/buildkit/Cargo.toml
!/containers/performance/buildkit/Cargo.toml

# The Blender run-container image (containers/blender/Dockerfile) COPYs its two
# authoring helpers — the importable glTF export module and the `tcab-blend`
# runner — straight from its own directory into the image (there is no cargo
# source to ride under !/crates for these). They live outside every path
# re-included above, so without this the COPYs fail with "not found" against the
# allowlisted context (transferring context: 2B).
!/containers/blender/tcab_blend_export.py
!/containers/blender/tcab-blend


# The performance image bakes the committed training scenarios from the
# Lattice case's version folder in the test-suites submodule (see
# containers/performance/Dockerfile's `COPY test-suites/test-cases/.../references/training`).
# It is the one test-case tree a build embeds, so it must survive the allowlist; the
# path tracks the Dockerfile's COPY, and admitting it is what has an image job
# initialize the test-suites submodule (scripts/ci/submodules.sh --build-context).
!/test-suites/test-cases/performance/hard/lattice/v1.0.0/references/training

# The web service image (deployments/images/web.Dockerfile) is the one NON-cargo
# service image: it `npm ci`s the npm workspace and `vite build`s the web console
# (apps/web) into static assets nginx serves. So its build context needs the npm
# workspace too — but only the slice the console build reads:
#   - the root npm manifests (package.json + the lockfile npm ci validates against),
#   - EVERY workspace member's package.json (npm ci validates the whole workspace
#     against the lockfile, so a missing member's manifest aborts the install), and
#   - the SOURCE of the packages the console actually bundles: apps/web itself
#     plus its workspace deps @clockwyrks/ui, @clockwyrks/run-record and
#     @clockwyrks/backend-api, and @clockwyrks/run-stats,
#     @clockwyrks/voxel-runtime, @clockwyrks/particle-runtime and
#     @clockwyrks/lattice-runtime, whose compiled subpaths the ui library
#     imports (run-stats' scoring rules and rollup; the voxel and particle
#     runtimes' `./three`; the lattice runtime's renderer and its
#     `lattice-core.wasm`).
#     The ui library and run-stats are the ui-common repository's, under
#     ui-common/packages/, and the runtimes the engines repository's, under
#     engines/packages/. Every one of them is consumed as its built dist/: the
#     Dockerfile builds each before the console — the sources ride along here,
#     dist/ is re-excluded below and rebuilt in-image.
# node_modules/ and dist/ are re-excluded below (those re-exclusions come last and
# win), so a local install/build never rides along.
!/package.json
!/package-lock.json
!/tsconfig.base.json
!/apps/docs/package.json
!/apps/site/package.json
!/apps/web
# The contracts packages extend the contracts repository's own base tsconfig.
!/contracts/tsconfig.base.json
!/contracts/packages/run-record
!/contracts/packages/backend-api
# The ui-common packages extend the ui-common repository's own base tsconfig.
!/ui-common/tsconfig.base.json
!/ui-common/packages/run-stats
!/ui-common/packages/ui
# The engines packages extend the engines repository's own base tsconfig.
!/engines/tsconfig.base.json
!/engines/packages/voxel-runtime
!/engines/packages/particle-runtime
!/engines/packages/lattice-runtime
# The rig/animation half of the generated contract. `run-record` re-exports it, so
# the console needs it for the same reason it needs `run-record`; separately, it is
# what the two runtimes above now depend on, so the staging script pulls it into the
# package store as part of their closure (scripts/ci/build-context.sh asserts this
# line exists for exactly that reason).
!/contracts/packages/asset-contract
# The engine runtimes are the odd ones out in this block: the console does not
# bundle them and the ui library does not import them. They ride along for the
# BASE image below, whose staging script bakes them into the package store beside
# the shippable packages — an engine runtime is seeded per run rather than named
# by a case, so each appears in that script's list and in no case manifest.
!/engines/packages/simple-2d
!/engines/packages/structured-2d
!/engines/packages/simple-3d
!/engines/packages/structured-3d
# The shared engineless (`none`) validator harness and canvas recorder. Nothing
# bundles it and no COPY names it: scripts/stage-tcab-packages.mjs bakes it into the
# package store beside the engine runtimes, and contracts/crates/suites' vitest validator
# (vitest_validator.rs, `stage_case_harness`) later copies its sources out of that
# store into a case's staged validator project. So the driver image must carry its
# sources, and a build context missing them fails inside the staging script
# ("shippable package @clockwyrks/case-harness not found ..."), taking every service
# image on services.Dockerfile down with it. scripts/ci/build-context.sh asserts every
# name in that script's SHIPPABLE list survives this allowlist, because a `COPY . .`
# stage is invisible to the COPY-source checks.
!/engines/packages/case-harness
# The run-container BASE image (containers/base/Dockerfile) reuses this same npm
# slice: its builder stage `npm ci`s the workspace and runs the staging script to
# bake the shippable @clockwyrks packages — and the simple-2d engine runtime —
# into /opt/tcab-packages (see
# containers/README.md#the-shippable-test-cabinet-packages). The script is the one
# extra file that build needs beyond the console's slice, so re-include it.
!/scripts/stage-tcab-packages.mjs
# THE gg SLICE the devcontainer image's gg layer (.devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile) stages
# from the gg checkout to install gg's program-language toolchains with gg's own
# installers: gg's scripts (the installers under scripts/ci, the shared shell they
# source, gg's build shell and the npm-delivered build tools' locks), the
# rust-toolchain.toml the Rust arm's pin defers to, and, file by file, every arm's pin
# and the files those pins read. The packages are named file by file, because a whole
# package would put the arms' sources into every `COPY . .` cache key; the installers
# read them by `$REPO_ROOT/<path>`, which scripts/ci/build-context.sh holds to this list.
# gg's scripts are one directory, named without a wildcard, so a new installer in gg
# needs no edit here.
!/gg/scripts
!/gg/rust-toolchain.toml
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-cpp/cpp-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-csharp/csharp-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-java/java-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-kotlin/kotlin-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/purescript-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.yaml
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.lock
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-python/build.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-python/requirements.txt
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-ruby/opal-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-ruby/yard-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/rust-version.sh
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/Cargo.toml
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/Cargo.lock
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox/guest/Cargo.toml
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox/guest/Cargo.lock
!/gg/packages/gg-sandbox-swift/swift-version.sh
# The CI scripts: scripts/ci/gg-release.sh with the shared shell it sources, which
# services.Dockerfile's `gg-release` stage bind-mounts to fetch the release the driver
# pins (the pin itself rides in under crates/).
!/scripts/ci
# This image is also the one service Dockerfile that COPYs sibling files from its
# own deployments/images/ directory (the nginx server block) — the Rust images embed
# nothing from here. They live outside every path re-included above, so without
# this the COPYs fail with "not found" against the allowlisted context.
!/deployments/images/web

# The DEVCONTAINER image (.devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile) builds from this same
# context, because its last layer installs gg's program-language toolchains with gg's
# own installers, from the gg slice admitted above, which read their pins from beside
# the arms under gg/packages/. Its own COPYs read these five paths and
# nothing else under .devcontainer/: README.md, devcontainer.json, the compose files,
# the Dockerfile and the host's .env are not admitted, and they churn harder than the
# scripts do. There is deliberately no sibling ubuntu.dockerfile.dockerignore: BuildKit
# would prefer one, Buildah (Podman, podman-compose, the devcontainer on a rootless
# Podman host) has been seen to read THIS file instead and die on the first COPY, so
# one allowlist serves every runtime. scripts/ci/build-context.sh checks the
# Dockerfile's COPYs against it. What it costs the seven `COPY . .` builds: their
# cache keys gain these ~30 small files, which move far less often than crates/.
!/.devcontainer/ai
!/.devcontainer/languages
!/.devcontainer/system
!/.devcontainer/tools
!/.devcontainer/post-install.sh

# Re-exclude build outputs and scratch that can appear UNDER the re-included
# paths, so a local `cargo build`'s target/ (or a stray node_modules) never bloats
# the context or churns the layer cache. These come last so they win.
**/target/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/.astro/
# The copies of committed case versions the Rust tests read. No image builds a test,
# and the copies weigh tens of megabytes.
/crates/*/testdata/
/engines/crates/*/testdata/
# TypeScript's incremental build state. `ui-common/packages/run-stats/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo`
# is gitignored output that lands inside `!/ui-common/packages/run-stats` — re-included for
# the web console image, which builds that package — and it is REWRITTEN by every local `tsc -b`. So a
# type-check on the host changes the context, which changes the `COPY . .` cache key, which
# recompiles the whole Rust workspace in an image that does not contain a line of
# TypeScript. As a glob rather than the one path, because the next package to gain a
# composite tsconfig should not have to rediscover this.
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# macOS Finder metadata, and the entry most likely to be the reason a `make images` you
# expected to be cached was not. `.DS_Store` files exist wherever a directory has been
# opened in Finder — this repository has them at its root and under crates/, packages/,
# scripts/, harnesses/, orchestrators/ and containers/, six of which sit inside allowlisted
# trees — and Finder rewrites them for reasons that have nothing to do with the repository:
# a window resized, a view mode changed, a folder scrolled. Each rewrite changes the
# `COPY . .` cache key of every build from this context, and the build stage's first act on
# a miss is to touch the tree and hand cargo a workspace it must compile from scratch. The
# symptom is a full ~19-crate rebuild with no source change, blamed on the Dockerfile.
#
# This is not hypothetical here: the primary development host is macOS running the tree
# through a Linux VM's shared mount, so Finder and the builder see the same directories.
**/.DS_Store

# The staged AUDIO STORE, and the one thing under dist/ that belongs in this context.
# The audio clips are not committed to this repository: they live in the private audio
# object store, and `containers/build.sh` runs scripts/stage-audio-store.mjs, which
# downloads and verifies every published pack into dist/audio-store/tree.
# containers/audio-store/Dockerfile then `COPY`s that directory (named through the
# AUDIO_STAGE_DIR build arg) to /opt/tcab-audio, which is the ONE image that carries
# audio: no RUN image bakes a pack any more, a run is staged with the packs its test
# case declares. The blanket `**/dist/` re-exclusion above would keep it out, so
# re-include just this subtree — and only it, so every OTHER dist/ (a local
# `npm run build`'s output) stays out as before. This re-inclusion has to come after
# `**/dist/` to win, which is why it lives here rather than with the allowlist proper.
#
# The pattern names the `tree` directory rather than the whole `dist/audio-store` root,
# which is as narrow as this file's format goes: it is exactly what the COPY reads, so
# the stager's content-addressed DOWNLOAD CACHE at dist/audio-store/.cache — a second
# copy of every clip it has ever verified, which no image reads — stays out, and so does
# anything else that ends up beside the tree.
!/dist/audio-store/tree
